                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_identity:      `<strong>That's the real diagnosis.</strong> This isn't a calendar problem — it's an identity-and-boundary problem. The belief that “responsive = valuable” is colliding with “present parent,” and you can't schedule your way out of who Priya thinks she is.`,
                    coach_r1_time:          `<strong>You've optimised her calendar and time-blocked the week — and she's still answering the CIO at 11pm.</strong> In her head, being responsive is who she is. You can't schedule your way out of an identity. What does Priya believe makes her valuable, and is that belief the thing burning her out?`,
                    coach_r1_effort:        `<strong>“Just push harder” is the trap itself.</strong> The gap is ~20 hours a week and it is structural — there is no amount of effort that closes it. That belief, “I can power through,” is exactly the always-on identity that has her sleeping under six hours.`,
                    coach_r1_client:        `<strong>NorthPeak isn't the root.</strong> The client is demanding, yes, but the conflict lives at Priya's boundary and in her identity, not in the CIO. Reframe this as a boundary-and-identity problem before you decide how to handle the client.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as an identity problem, designed for her real integration preference, AND located the threatened “always-available star” identity. That is exactly the lens that lets you build a plan that survives week one instead of fighting Priya's own style.`,
                    coach_r1_hardseg:       `<strong>You've prescribed hard separation — laptop off, phone in a drawer.</strong> But Priya actually likes weaving short bursts of work into her evening and resents rigid walls. A plan that fights her preference won't survive week one. Are you designing for Priya's style, or imposing yours?`,
                    coach_r1_skills:        `<strong>This isn't a competence gap.</strong> Priya was a star IC and is a capable manager — telling her she “lacks skills” adds a second identity threat and misses the real one: the belief that her value is being always available.`,
                    coach_r1_nothreat:      `<strong>“Just overloaded for three weeks” underplays it.</strong> The reason she'll answer at 11pm even after you fix her calendar is identity, not load. Name the threatened identity — “the always-available star” — or the behaviour comes straight back.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>Low honesty is how this stays a calendar exercise.</strong> If Priya won't admit the identity is the issue, she'll time-block her week and quietly keep answering at midnight. Name it plainly or you'll repeat the burnout.`,
                    coach_r2_coherent:      `<strong>That's a coherent boundary plan.</strong> You spread across families, delegated the stand-up, and led with an explicit renegotiation — that closes the structural gap instead of just shifting demand around. Tactics that reinforce each other are what survive contact with the sprint.`,
                    coach_r2_spread:        `<strong>A decent spread across families.</strong> You've avoided the grab-bag, but lean harder on delegation and the explicit client reset — those are what actually take hours off the table rather than rearranging them.`,
                    coach_r2_grabbag:       `<strong>This is a grab-bag, not a plan.</strong> Loading nearly everything into one family leaves the gap mostly open. Kreiner's point is a coherent set across behavioural, temporal, physical and communicative — each making the others credible.`,
                    coach_r2_nodelegate:    `<strong>You barely delegated.</strong> Under-delegation is a primary driver of Priya's 20-hour gap — the 7am stand-up and a deputy lead are exactly what should come off her plate. You can't hold a boundary around work you refuse to hand over.`,
                    coach_r2_nocomm:        `<strong>You built walls but never renegotiated.</strong> Temporal and physical tactics with no explicit reset just push the same demand to a different hour. The communicative family — resetting “real-time” with the CIO — is what actually changes the demand.`,
                    coach_r2_workable:      `<strong>A workable, written cadence is the keystone.</strong> One 8am brief and a same-day reply window resets “real-time” to something Priya can sustain — and putting it in writing makes the boundary credible rather than wishful.`,
                    coach_r2_realtime:      `<strong>Accepting “real-time” signs the burnout contract.</strong> If the CIO can reach her at any hour, every other boundary is decorative. The single most important reset is turning “real-time” into a workable, explicit cadence.`,
                    coach_r2_silent:        `<strong>Quietly going dark is the worst of both.</strong> Without an explicit reset, the CIO just reads it as Priya becoming unreliable — you get the relationship cost of a boundary with none of the credibility of having actually set one.`,
                    coach_r2_unenforced:    `<strong>Priya told the client “no calls after 7pm” and answered at 8:15.</strong> Now the boundary means less than if she'd never set it — she's taught NorthPeak her limits are negotiable. Which limits can she actually hold under pressure, and what makes them credible?`,
                    coach_r3_judgement:     `<strong>That's the identity work.</strong> Re-anchoring her value on judgement, outcomes and leading a team decouples “valuable” from “always available” — the durable fix. Change the behaviour without changing the identity and it snaps back; change the identity and the behaviour follows.`,
                    coach_r3_keep:          `<strong>Keeping the “most responsive” identity keeps the disease.</strong> Leaning into it feels like commitment but it's the exact belief burning her out. As long as her worth equals her availability, no boundary plan will hold past the first hard week.`,
                    coach_r3_withdraw:      `<strong>“Stepping back” swaps one failure for another.</strong> Disengaging protects Priya but puts the NorthPeak relationship she personally owns at risk. The goal is a redefined value, not a retreat — judgement and outcomes, delivered, not abandoned.`,
                    coach_r3_bossreframe:   `<strong>That resists the label without reading as less committed.</strong> Thanking him and re-anchoring on outcomes reframes the praise instead of rejecting it — she keeps her standing while quietly refusing the “responsiveness machine” identity.`,
                    coach_r3_blunt:         `<strong>“I'm not your responsiveness machine” — and the boss hears “less committed than I thought.”</strong> The identity threat is real, but rejecting it bluntly costs her standing. Reframe her value — judgement, outcomes, leading a team — so she resists the label without sounding like she's checking out.`,
                    coach_r3_bossaccept:    `<strong>“Responsive is who I am” locks the trap shut.</strong> Accepting the boss's framing gratefully cements the exact identity that has her awake at 11pm. The praise is a hook — take it and you've agreed to keep burning.`,
                    coach_r3_promise:       `<strong>“One more time, I promise” is what was said last crunch.</strong> Asking the partner to absorb it again — without a concrete plan — is why they said “I can't absorb another one of these.” A second consecutive episode is the strongest predictor she quits. Share the load for real.`,
                    coach_r3_avoid:         `<strong>Avoiding the home conversation doesn't make it go away.</strong> The daycare gap is real and the partner is already at their limit. Managing it silently means Priya absorbs it all — straight back into the 20-hour gap and the burnout.`,
                    coach_r3_nononneg:      `<strong>“Stay flexible so nothing is ever refused” is no boundary at all.</strong> A boundary you'll always bend isn't credible to anyone, including Priya. Pick one realistic non-negotiable and hold it — that's what teaches the system her limits are real.`,
                    coach_r3_overpromise:   `<strong>Two full weekend days off mid-sprint is an over-promise.</strong> A non-negotiable she can't keep becomes another broken boundary the first hard weekend. Choose something realistic — a protected bedtime hour she can hold every night beats a grand promise she can't.`,
                    coach_r4_cadence:       `<strong>Holding the cadence is the whole point.</strong> A brief acknowledgement, confirmed at 8am, honours the boundary AND the client — the issue genuinely waits, and NorthPeak learns her limits are real, not negotiable. That's a boundary that survives contact with pressure.`,
                    coach_r4_cave:          `<strong>You caved at 9pm and worked it to midnight.</strong> One break under pressure and the agreed cadence is dead — you've re-taught the CIO that “urgent” means Priya appears at any hour. The boundary that bends every time it's tested was never a boundary.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Going dark on a real board-deadline risk swaps one failure for another.</strong> Priya protected her evening but NorthPeak may miss its deadline and the relationship she owns is now at risk. Well-being and performance aren't a binary — this was the moment for a deliberate, bounded exception.`,
                    coach_r4_bounded:       `<strong>That's bounded flexibility, not erosion.</strong> A named, time-boxed exception for a genuine board-deadline risk protects both well-being and performance — and because it's announced as the exception, it doesn't dissolve the rule. That's permeability you control.`,
                    coach_r4_never:         `<strong>Total segmentation creates a new failure.</strong> Never flexing, even for a real board-deadline risk, protects home perfectly while the client relationship and sprint fail. A deliberate, bounded exception is different from caving — learn the difference.`,
                    coach_r4_alwaysflex:    `<strong>Flexing every time it's awkward IS erosion.</strong> If every uncomfortable moment becomes an exception, you don't have a boundary — you have a suggestion. Bounded flexibility means rare, named, deliberate exceptions, not a standing escape hatch.`,
                    coach_r4_solo:          `<strong>You cured one person and left the disease in the team.</strong> Priya fixed her own boundaries, but at 10pm her status still shows “active,” so the seven reports stay on too. How does her boundary become a norm that protects all of them, not a private arrangement?`,
                    coach_r4_tellthem:      `<strong>“You set boundaries” while she answers at 11pm teaches the opposite.</strong> The team models what Priya does, not what she says. Telling them to log off while her own light is on at 11pm just confirms the always-on norm.`,
                    coach_r4_leadlag:       `<strong>Leading plus lagging indicators is how she'll know it held.</strong> Sleep and after-hours pings warn her early; sprint milestones and turnover-risk confirm it later. Watch both and she can course-correct before the next surge instead of reading it in an exit interview.`,
                    coach_r4_noindicators:  `<strong>“If it feels fine, assume it's working” is how this quietly decays.</strong> Without leading indicators Priya won't see the boundary slipping until the next pulse survey or a resignation. Name what she'll watch, this week and over the sprint.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed identity & boundary; designed for her real style.`,
